`Deck.shuffle()`/`deal()` and `Hand.addCard()` are the entire contract any card game builds on.

```java
class Deck {
    private final List<Card> cards;

    void shuffle() { Collections.shuffle(cards); }

    Card deal() {
        if (cards.isEmpty()) throw new IllegalStateException("deck is empty");
        return cards.remove(cards.size() - 1);
    }
}

class Hand {
    private final List<Card> cards = new ArrayList<>();
    void addCard(Card c) { cards.add(c); }
    List<Card> getCards() { return List.copyOf(cards); }
}
```

Notice `Hand` has no `value()` or `isBust()` method — those are Blackjack concepts, added by a `BlackjackHand` subtype or a separate scorer, not baked into the generic contract.

`Hand.value()` applies the downgrade loop from the previous slide — no per-hand special casing.

```java
int value() {
    int total = 0;
    int aceCount = 0;
    for (Card c : cards) {
        total += c.getRank().hardValue(); // Ace=11, face=10, else pip value
        if (c.getRank() == Rank.ACE) aceCount++;
    }
    while (total > 21 && aceCount > 0) {
        total -= 10;
        aceCount--;
    }
    return total;
}
```

This same loop correctly scores a hand with zero, one, two, or four Aces — the number of Aces only changes how many times the `while` loop can fire, never the logic itself.
